<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yusuf al-Azma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yusuf al-Azma was a Syrian army officer and war minister of the Arab Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Trained in the Ottoman army, he refused to accept the French ultimatum of July 1920 without a fight and led a small force against the French at Maysalun, where he was killed on 24 July 1920. He is honoured in Syria as a national hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-al-azma.jpg" alt="Yusuf al-Azma" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yusuf_al-Azma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yusuf al-Azma was born in 1883 into a prominent mercantile and landowning family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> descent, whose members belonged to the Ottoman Syrian establishment; his brother Aziz Bey was a district governor and many relatives were Ottoman officers. He graduated from the Ottoman Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> in 1906, was a member of the reformist Committee of Union and Progress, and trained further in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> until 1909, before serving as military attaché in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. He married a Turkish woman, with whom he had a daughter, Laila.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> al-Azma commanded the Ottoman 25th Infantry Division in 1914 and later served as a deputy to War Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a>. The historian Michael Provence notes that, contrary to a widely held belief, he did not join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a> but remained a decorated Ottoman frontline officer until October 1918, when Emir Faisal's Sharifian Army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. He then returned to the city, joined the Arab nationalist society al-Fatat and became Faisal's personal chamberlain. On 26 January 1920 he was made chief of general staff as well as minister of war.</p><p>He gathered weapons left by the Ottoman Army, raised funds for more and by mid-1920 had built a force of some 10,000 men, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> volunteers and former Ottoman officers. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> a mandate over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in April 1920, he led the majority in the government that rejected French rule, against Rida al-Rikabi's faction, which sought compromise. On 13 July he declared emergency measures in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-national-congress">Syrian National Congress</a>, among them press censorship, although Yasin al-Hashimi judged the army woefully unprepared: each soldier would have 270 bullets and each gun eight shells.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 18 July al-Azma was the only minister to oppose the cabinet's decision to avoid war. At Maysalun, some 12 miles west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, he led the central column of a few hundred regulars and around 1,000 volunteers, armed largely with old rifles and 15 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>. Most resistance collapsed within the first hour, and he was shot by machine-gun fire from a French tank, the only Arab officer to die in the battle.</p><p>The historian Phillip S. Khoury writes that Syrians immortalised him as the supreme national martyr. His statue stands in a square named after him in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and streets and schools across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> bear his name. His tomb at Maysalun, built in the 1930s, is a stone sarcophagus engraved with the Zulfiqar sword, where the Syrian military honours him each year on Maysalun Day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yusuf al-Azma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>France sent an ultimatum, and on 24 July 1920 its army defeated the Syrian force at Maysalun, where the war minister <b>Yusuf al-Azma</b> was killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/arab-kingdom-of-syria">Arab Kingdom of Syria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in Maysalun, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Damascus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yusuf al-Azma"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5448 4687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5448 4687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
